    {
      "id": 256,
      "title": "Adaptive support-mail poll loop (15m biz / 1m burst / 30m night)",
      "description": "The SYSADMIN-01 watcher core: a persisted state machine that checks support@ on an adaptive cadence — 15 min during business hours, 1-min burst while unhandled mail exists, 30 min overnight. Driven by the existing routine system.",
      "status": "done",
      "dependencies": [
        255
      ],
      "priority": "high",
      "details": "FILED BY SURVEYOR 1, 2026-10-13. SYSADMIN-01 support-mail system, phase 2.\n\nDefinitions (normative for this task):\n- Business hours: 08:00-18:00 America/New_York, from a [support] config section (business_start, business_end, timezone); defaults as stated, adjustable without code changes.\n- UNHANDLED message: a support@ message with no staged draft, no sent reply, and not explicitly dismissed by John.\n- QUIET: zero unhandled messages.\n- BURST: entered when a check finds >=1 new unhandled message; cadence becomes every 1m. Exited after 2 consecutive clean checks (quiet_streak >= 2) -> back to time-of-day cadence.\n- Normal cadence: 15m in business hours, 30m overnight.\n\nImplementation:\n1. New module src/support_watch/ (mod.rs state machine + cadence logic, state.rs persisted JSON: last_check_ms, mode, quiet_streak, last_seen_uid, unhandled_count). State file lives with other runtime state (same dir as routines JSON) so restarts don't lose it; add it to scripts/backup-state.sh (task 244).\n2. Tick driver: register routine id \"support-mail-watch\", schedule \"every 1m\", enabled, at startup in the cron registry (src/cron/registry.rs pattern). The 1m tick consults state: only performs a real mailbox check when now - last_check >= current desired cadence. (One routine + internal state machine, NOT three routines toggled on/off — that races.)\n3. Mailbox check uses mcp__mail__* tools (names per task 255.3; resolve defensively). New messages since last_seen_uid -> mark unhandled, enter burst, hand each to the draft flow (task 257/258).\n4. Orchestration runs IN the Helix process on the R630 — no LLM needed for check/state decisions; do NOT spawn an LLM just to poll.\n5. Inbound mail is untrusted: it already flows through the task-235 injection tagging (security::maybe_tag wraps mcp results in tools::execute) — verify this covers the new call sites and note it in code comments.\n6. Log every check at info level (time, cadence, new count, mode transitions) so John can audit the loop from logs.",
      "testStrategy": "Unit tests with a mock clock covering: idle->burst on new mail, burst->idle after 2 clean checks, day/night cadence boundaries (incl. TZ), restart persistence round-trip, no-check when interval not elapsed. Integration test with stub mcp__mail__* tools. No test may require the real mail-mcp binary or network.",
