      "description": "John's deploy-time check on the bot VM: non-destructive read-only probe of existing ASUS SSH reachability. Decides whether task 9 is needed at all.",
      "status": "pending",
      "dependencies": [],
      "priority": "high",
      "details": "Run FIRST, before any ASUS setup work, on the bot VM as cobble. Non-destructive, read-only, and NEVER print key material (filenames + exit codes only, never key contents). Commands: (1) command -v ssh (client present). (2) ls -l ~/.ssh/ (filenames only — look for keypairs + config). (3) grep -i 'asus|microtech|100.112.110.14|192.168.1.196' ~/.ssh/config (config entries — host aliases/users, no key material). (4) known_hosts hits WITHOUT printing keys (exit code only): ssh-keygen -F 100.112.110.14 -f ~/.ssh/known_hosts > /dev/null 2>&1 && echo TAILNET_KNOWN || echo TAILNET_UNKNOWN, and the same for 192.168.1.196 (LAN_KNOWN/UNKNOWN). (5) PROBE — only if an identity is configured (a ~/.ssh/config Host entry naming a user/key) or John supplies the Windows username for this probe: ssh -o BatchMode=yes -o ConnectTimeout=15 <user>@100.112.110.14 hostname. BatchMode fails fast on a missing key instead of hanging on a password prompt; hostname is read-only and harmless. If a config Host alias exists for the ASUS, probe via the alias instead. If no identity is configured and John does not supply a username, the probe cannot run — record that (verdict NOT-REACHABLE, reason 'unknown user'). VERDICT (record in writing): REACHABLE = the probe printed a hostname (record which user/host/key-alias worked — no key material) — Phase 2 has NO setup dependency, SKIP task 9 entirely. NOT-REACHABLE = no configured identity, or probe failed (permission denied / timeout / unreachable) — task 9 applies. NOTE: a failed probe does NOT prove the ASUS SSH server is absent (it may be a missing key only) — the fallback setup covers both cases. This task may run immediately, in parallel with the Phase 1 build.",
