      "priority": "high",
      "details": "Extend [chat_command] config: asus_enabled (default false — John enables after setup/verify), asus_host (REQUIRED, no default; current documented value 100.112.110.14), asus_user (REQUIRED), asus_key_path (REQUIRED, e.g. ~/.ssh/id_helix_asus — path only, never key material). Router: after '!' strip + trim, if the remainder starts with 'asus:' (exact lowercase) then target = ASUS, command = rest trimmed; else target = local. If target = ASUS and (!asus_enabled or any asus_* unset) then reply 'ASUS relay not enabled/configured' (fail closed, NO ssh attempted). The same sender gate (task 3) runs BEFORE routing; the same allowlist + metachar rejection (task 4) runs BEFORE ssh — this is what makes remote-shell invocation safe (ssh runs the remote command via the user's shell; with metachars rejected, the remote shell sees only simple words). SSH invocation via std::process::Command (no shell on the R630 side either): argv = ssh -o BatchMode=yes -o ConnectTimeout=15 -i <key_path> <user>@<host> <command words...>; command timeout = command_timeout_secs covering the whole ssh call. Capture stdout+stderr like task 4. Non-zero exit / timeout / unreachable posts an error back (never silent, never hang: BatchMode + ConnectTimeout + command timeout are all mandatory). Reply labeled: first line 'asus$ <command>', chunks '[n/m] ' like task 5, to the same room. This build task does NOT depend on task 8's outcome (the code is identical either way); only the live drill (task 12) depends on connectivity.",
      "testStrategy": "Unit tests: routing parse (local vs ASUS, case sensitivity, 'asus:' with no command gives an error reply); ASUS disabled/unconfigured gives zero ssh attempts (assert via stub ssh on PATH recording invocations); allowlist-before-ssh (disallowed ASUS command gives a rejection reply, zero ssh calls); metachar ASUS command gives a rejection, zero ssh calls; ssh failure/timeout gives an error reply.",
